{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791143873,"nonce":"eYQwltNUuqEa1t0ODNagetzLVqQ=","title":"an empty macOS poll can block for part of a display frame","message":"Since 39adb4a8ca43084e857a95437d4a2fd381ef0a66, when the monitor reaches a poll's stop event it fetches without waiting, which takes in the window server's events (ad76070). After that nested fetch, the next poll's stop event, posted before -[NSApplication run] starts, isn't dequeued until the display cycle. Back-to-back empty polls average 5 ms each (75 Hz external display), all of it before the monitor runs; the monitor itself takes under 0.2 ms. The time is spent blocked in mach_msg, not CPU.\n\nMeasured (pollbench, 500 empty polls):\n  without the nested fetch (e505c4a): 0.06 ms each, but a poll-only program gets no window-server events\n  nested -[NSApp nextEventMatchingMask:untilDate:distantPast ... dequeue:NO]: 5.0 ms\n  a plain CFRunLoopRunInMode(default, 0, true) in its place: 8.4 ms\n\n8d581e7 polls also took up to a frame, and loops that pace on vsync don't notice (ashui: 1% CPU idle; hlwindow gpu_test 349 frames in 4 s). Worth finding how to take in window-server events without delaying the next stop event, for example by fetching only when the window has had no run-loop pass for some milliseconds.","files":null}]}